        {
            "number": 2,
            "revision": "6316c7a3287ac3ca327cf12e24a52933349cef56",
            "parents": [
                "4509e841f6a4cad2411aa00db7cefac52330f9d2"
            ],
            "ref": "refs/changes/96/88596/2",
            "uploader": {
                "name": "Alexander",
                "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                "username": "pointbazaar"
            },
            "createdOn": 1775587249,
            "author": {
                "name": "Alexander",
                "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                "username": "pointbazaar"
            },
            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Marc Olberding",
                        "email": "molberding@nvidia.com",
                        "username": "molbear"
                    },
                    "message": "I'm fine with the change as is. as a matter of long term discussion, do we want to think about some structural changes that make changes like this less impactful to the ABI? We're pulling a decent amount of \"stuff\" into the \"ABI\" that is more or less non-functional from an ABI compatibility perspective."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "Agree that the amount of diff in the ABI dump is rather large.\n\nI hope (and expect) it will be less in future changes.\n\n```\nwc -l  abi/x86_64/libpldm++/gcc.dump \n3806 abi/x86_64/libpldm++/gcc.dump\nwc -l  abi/x86_64/gcc.dump \n15248 abi/x86_64/gcc.dump\n```\n\nWhat is your idea to reduce the ABI surface ?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "@andrew@codeconstruct.com.au\n\nneed your review for the CHANGELOG.md update"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Marc Olberding",
                        "email": "molberding@nvidia.com",
                        "username": "molbear"
                    },
                    "message": "A lot of my complaint here is the cruft that comes in that isn't really directly part of the interface proper...type traits, allocator traits, internal redblack trees.\n\n\nI agree that I would expect the diffs in size to slow down, but that's really more of a measure of the amount of stuff in the C++ standard that hasn't been made part of our ABI yet \ud83d\ude0a. \n\nI wonder if it makes sense to filter things that come from internal headers. Maybe we can filter off of something like:\n```\n      'NameSpace' => 'std',\n      'PrivateABI' => 1,\n```\n\n\nmy point being that I don't really care if one of the std library symbols gets brought in, really only if the function signatures on our public API changes, afaict we're not terribly worried about true ABI breakages (this was an argument I made against using C++ for this, and andrew informed me that we're not terribly worried about 100% ABI compatibility).\n\nI guess I'd like @andrew@codeconstruct.com.au's input on this, when he's able. It isn't terribly pressing, but the amount of stuff coming into gcc dump's on the two major commits so far make it more of a maintenance burden than it is a tool to spot breakages.\n\nIIRC since our main distribution method for this library is part of a unified deployment model, does it make sense at some point to just make this a header only library? That seems to be how 90% of C++ projects deal with this problem. This clearly isn't a short term solution. The bonus here is that it absolves us from needing to worry about backwards compat in a super meaningful way, there's no stale .so sitting around to break for the C++ interface. The con is of course, everything normally involved in a header only lib."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "Header-only design for libpldm++ was considered initially\nhttps://discordapp.com/channels/775381525260664832/1287936533491613798/1412615316034420879\n\nand rejected, but i will leave this open in case anyone wants to further explore the tradeoffs.\n\nFrom my side, i would like to rely on Andrew's experience when it comes to what's the best way to distribute a library.\n\nIf the dump is too much noise, can we develop better tooling / filter the dump?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Marc Olberding",
                        "email": "molberding@nvidia.com",
                        "username": "molbear"
                    },
                    "message": "hah, fair enough. Filtering out internal C++ headers seems like the way to go in my mind, but andrew is probably the right person to chime in on this."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Jeffery",
                        "email": "andrew@codeconstruct.com.au",
                        "username": "amboar"
                    },
                    "message": "> A lot of my complaint here is the cruft that comes in that isn't really directly part of the interface proper...type traits, allocator traits, internal redblack trees.\n\nThe problem is, at the ABI level, their structure matters, and so the details must be included in the dump to evaluate for problems.\n\n> It isn't terribly pressing, but the amount of stuff coming into gcc dump's on the two major commits so far make it more of a maintenance burden than it is a tool to spot breakages.\n\nIt's not my intent that the dump be visually inspected in Gerrit reviews to identify defects. For the most part I recommend skipping it. Historically, I've just wanted it recorded. `abi-compliance-checker` is the tool to use to understand problems, and this is run as part of CI. Though in CI it will just fail and give binary feedback, running it locally gives a HTML report of what's changed, the severity of the problem, and explanations for why the change is problematic.\n\nGiven that, I don't see it either as either possible or necessary to filter the dump, its integrity is important to the function of `abi-compliance-checker`.\n\nThat said, with some work to the CI scripts in the repo it might be possible to avoid recording the dump files to start with."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "Is there any concrete steps i need to take on this patch.\n\nDo i need to develop new ABI tooling to stabilize these symbols?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Jeffery",
                        "email": "andrew@codeconstruct.com.au",
                        "username": "amboar"
                    },
                    "message": "Left a comment about handling pins."
                }
            ],
