        {
            "number": 3,
            "revision": "fa8e42c265889c8a4049480f23c7da5407b552d5",
            "parents": [
                "bd309767beb6f32e9380b3f259566c1c804b1326"
            ],
            "ref": "refs/changes/96/88596/3",
            "uploader": {
                "name": "Alexander",
                "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                "username": "pointbazaar"
            },
            "createdOn": 1776696808,
            "author": {
                "name": "Alexander",
                "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                "username": "pointbazaar"
            },
            "kind": "TRIVIAL_REBASE",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "bindings/cpp/firmware_update.cpp",
                    "line": 111,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Jeffery",
                        "email": "andrew@codeconstruct.com.au",
                        "username": "amboar"
                    },
                    "message": "My remaining concern here is that the instances of `struct pldm_package_format_pin` represent the extensible _C_ structs, which are distinct from the extensible C++ structs. The value is related to but also separate from the spec'ed package format revisions, so I don't think it's appropriate to mix them in this way. I'd rather develop equivalent pins for the C++ API so it can evolve independently of the C API (even if it's implemented in terms of the C API)."
                },
                {
                    "file": "bindings/cpp/firmware_update.cpp",
                    "line": 111,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "> * @param[in] pin The maximum supported package format revision of the caller\n\nWhat if we dropped this PIN argument entirely from the C++ API.\n\nI can imagine many callers are not terribly interest in specific revisions\nand instead just need to know if a given fields exists in the libpldm++ they are linked against.\n\nFor that purpose we have `HasMember` function from `GrowableStruct`.\n\nCurrently they don't need to use that though since we only support v1.0 for now.\n\n\n> I'd rather develop equivalent pins for the C++ API so it can evolve independently of the C API\n\nWith 'equivalent' you mean copy-pasting it with a different name?\n\nOr should we start with e.g. `LIBPLDMPP_PKG_PIN_1` and then increment from there and translate internally to some libpldm pin."
                },
                {
                    "file": "bindings/cpp/firmware_update.cpp",
                    "line": 111,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Jeffery",
                        "email": "andrew@codeconstruct.com.au",
                        "username": "amboar"
                    },
                    "message": "> For that purpose we have HasMember function from GrowableStruct.\n\nI guess it's up to you as the maintainer of the C++ API, but I feel that might not be nice to write against. I think it would be better to have a one-off check to handle whether the library supports the requested version.\n\n> With 'equivalent' you mean copy-pasting it with a different name?\n\nNot necessarily. The C pin implementation is my own paranoid scheme, I'm not going to require that you copy it. The idea is that the library implementation can determine the minimum source version the user was compiled against.\n\n> Or should we start with e.g. LIBPLDMPP_PKG_PIN_1 and then increment from there and translate internally to some libpldm pin.\n\nI'm okay with that if that's what you prefer. Essentially I just made the underlying value of that C pin a constant relevant to the structs at hand and slightly awkward to calculate so people didn't just plug a literal constant in as the parameter. You could use a scoped enumeration to the same end (so people have to pass the enum value, not just a value of the underlying type)."
                },
                {
                    "file": "bindings/cpp/firmware_update.cpp",
                    "line": 111,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "Thanks for explaining it for me \ud83d\udc4d\n\nI went with a scoped enumeration for now and added comments as to what it does.\n\nAlso added comments to all structs members documenting in which version of the struct they were introduced."
                }
            ],
